   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to extending eligibility, for an additional three years, for the exemption of certain new or substantially rehabilitated multiple dwellings from local taxation",
    "basePrintNo": "S2314",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2314-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §421-m, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S2314         REVISED MEMO 01/29/2015\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the real property tax law, in relation\nto extending eligibility, for an additional three years, for the\nexemption of certain new or substantially rehabilitated multiple\ndwellings from local taxation\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo continue the real property tax law 421-m exemption program for an\nadditional three year period.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill amends paragraph (b) of subdivision 3 of\nsection 421-m of the real property tax law\n\nSection two provides an immediate effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSection 421-m of the Real Property Tax law is a new program which was\nestablished by Chapter 47 of the laws of 2011. It authorizes most\nmunicipalities outside of New York City, at local option, to enact a\nlocal law to offer a real property tax incentive to encourage the\ndevelopment of affordable housing and the remediation of blighted or\nunderutilized areas within a city, town or village.\n\nUnder 421-m, the municipality may designate a development area in\nwhich multiple dwellings and mixed use development consisting of 50%\nor more residential floor space, will be eligible for the program if\nthey contain a minimum of 20% affordable housing and have been\nconstructed on formerly blighted, unsanitary, vacant, or underutilized\nproperty. In order to ensure continued eligibility for the exemption,\ntenants may not pay more than 30% of their income in rent.\n\nThe program provides a unique opportunity for localities to encourage\ndevelopment, especially in dilapidated downtown areas which require\nincentive development to bring residents back to main street areas to\nhelp revitalize them. The program is schedule to end on June 15, 2015\nand in order to take advantage of the program, a municipality must\nenact a local law and construction on any eligible project must begin\nby that date.\n\nUnfortunately, 421-m has not yet been utilized by a locality. Part of\nthe reason for this is that the New York State Division of Housing has\nonly recently developed the draft regulations which are necessary for\na municipality to develop an income certification and monitoring plan\nas directed by the statute. Extension of the program for another three\nyears will afford more time for municipal governments to institute the\nnecessary planning and development strategies, to pass a local law\nimplementing the program, and to have their certification and income\nmonitoring plans approved by the New York State Division of Housing.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2013, 2014: S.5318 Passed the Senate\n\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
